{
  "title": "Limits and Continuity — Theory and Exercise 15.4",
  "notes": [
    "<h2>Limits and Continuity — Theory</h2>",

    "<h3>Right Hand Limit</h3>",
    "<p>A function $f(x)$ is said to have right hand limit $l_1$ at $x=a$ as $x$ approaches $a$ through values greater than $a$ (i.e. $x$ approaches $a$ from right). It is written as:</p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x) = l_1$$",

    "<h3>Left Hand Limit</h3>",
    "<p>A function $f(x)$ is said to have left hand limit $l_2$ at $x=a$ as $x$ approaches $a$ through values less than $a$ (i.e. $x$ approaches $a$ from left). It is written as:</p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = l_2$$",

    "<h3>Continuity</h3>",
    "<p>A function $f(x)$ at $x=a$ is said to be continuous iff:</p>",
    "<p>i) $f(a)$ exists</p>",
    "$$\\text{ii) } \\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a} f(x)$$",
    "$$\\text{iii) } f(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} f(a)$$",
    "<p>Otherwise, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=a$.</p>",

    "<h3>Note</h3>",
    "<p>A necessary and sufficient condition for $f(x)$ to have a limit at $x=a$ is:</p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x)$$",

    "<h2>Exercise 15.4</h2>",

    "<h3>Question 1: Test the continuity or discontinuity of the following functions by calculating the left-hand limits, the right-hand limits and the values of the functions at points specified</h3>",

    "<h4>i) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = x^2 \\text{ at } x = 4$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$f(4) = 4^2 = 16$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 4^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 4^-} x^2 = 4^2 = 16$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} x^2 = 4^2 = 16$$",
    "<p>Since $\\lim_{x \\to 4^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} f(x) = f(4)$, it is continuous.</p>",

    "<h4>ii) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = 2 - 3x^2 \\text{ at } x = 0$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$f(0) = 2 - 3 \\cdot 0^2 = 2$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 0^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 0^-} (2-3x^2) = 2 - 3 \\cdot 0^2 = 2$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 0^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 0^+} (2-3x^2) = 2 - 3 \\cdot 0^2 = 2$$",
    "<p>Hence, $\\lim_{x \\to 0} f(x) = f(0)$. So, it is continuous at $x=0$.</p>",

    "<h4>iii) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = 3x^2 - 2x + 4 \\text{ at } x = 1$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$f(1) = 3 \\cdot 1^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 + 4 = 5$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 1^-} (3x^2-2x+4) = 3 \\cdot 1^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 + 4 = 5$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 1^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 1^+} (3x^2-2x+4) = 3 \\cdot 1^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 + 4 = 5$$",
    "<p>Hence, $\\lim_{x \\to 1} f(x) = f(1)$. So, it is continuous at $x=1$.</p>",

    "<h4>iv) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\frac{1}{2x} \\text{ at } x = 0$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$f(0) = \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot 0} = \\frac{1}{0} = \\infty \\text{, doesn't exist}$$",
    "<p>Since $f(0)$ doesn't exist, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=0$.</p>",

    "<h4>v) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x-2} \\text{ at } x \\ne 2$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b> Let $x = a \\ne 2$:</p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^-} \\frac{1}{x-2} = \\frac{1}{a-2}$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} \\frac{1}{x-2} = \\frac{1}{a-2}$$",
    "$$f(a) = \\frac{1}{a-2}$$",
    "<p>$\\therefore f(x)$ is continuous at $x=a \\ne 2$, i.e. $x \\ne 2$.</p>",

    "<h4>vi) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\frac{1}{3x} \\text{ at } x \\ne 0$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b> Let $x = a \\ne 0$:</p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^-} \\frac{1}{3x} = \\frac{1}{3a}$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} \\frac{1}{3x} = \\frac{1}{3a}$$",
    "$$f(a) = \\frac{1}{3a}$$",
    "<p>Hence, $f(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x)$. So, it is continuous at $x \\ne 0$.</p>",

    "<h4>vii) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\frac{1}{1-x} \\text{ at } x = 1$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 1^-} \\frac{1}{1-x}, \\quad f(1) = \\frac{1}{1-1} = \\frac{1}{0} = \\infty$$",
    "<p>Here, $f(1)$ doesn't exist. So, it is discontinuous at $x=1$.</p>",

    "<h4>viii) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x-3} \\text{ at } x = 3$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$f(3) = \\frac{1}{3-3} = \\frac{1}{0} = \\infty$$",
    "<p>Here, $f(3)$ doesn't exist. So, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=3$.</p>",

    "<h4>ix) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\frac{x^2-9}{x-3} \\text{ at } x = 3$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 3^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 3^-} \\frac{x^2-9}{x-3} = \\lim_{x \\to 3^-} \\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{(x-3)} = \\lim_{x \\to 3^-} (x+3) = 3+3 = 6$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 3^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 3^+} \\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{(x-3)} = 3+3 = 6$$",
    "<p>Also, $f(3) = \\dfrac{3^2-9}{3-3} = \\dfrac{0}{0}$; here $f(3)$ doesn't exist. So, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=3$.</p>",

    "<h4>x) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\frac{x^2-16}{x-4} \\text{ at } x = 4$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 4^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 4^-} \\frac{x^2-16}{x-4} = \\lim_{x \\to 4^-} (x+4) = 4+4 = 8$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} (x+4) = 4+4 = 8$$",
    "<p>Also, $f(4) = \\dfrac{4^2-16}{4-4} = \\dfrac{0}{0}$; here $f(4)$ doesn't exist. So, it is discontinuous at $x=4$.</p>",

    "<h4>xi) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\frac{|x-2|}{x-2} \\text{ at } x = 2$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} \\frac{|x-2|}{x-2} = \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} \\frac{-(x-2)}{x-2} = -1$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} \\frac{|x-2|}{x-2} = \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} \\frac{(x-2)}{x-2} = 1$$",
    "<p>Here, $\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) \\ne \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x)$. So, it is discontinuous at $x=2$.</p>",

    "<h4>xii) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\frac{x}{|x|} \\text{ at } x = 0$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 0^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 0^-} \\frac{x}{-x} = \\frac{0}{0}$$",
    "<p>Here, $\\lim_{x \\to 0} f(x)$ doesn't exist. So, it is discontinuous at $x=0$.</p>",

    "<h3>Question 2: Discuss the continuity of functions at the points specified</h3>",

    "<h4>i) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\begin{cases} 2-x^2 & x \\le 2 \\\\ x-4 & x > 2 \\end{cases} \\text{ at } x = 2$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} (2-x^2) = 2 - 2^2 = -2$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} (x-4) = 2-4 = -2$$",
    "$$f(2) = 2 - 2^2 = -2$$",
    "<p>Here, $\\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x) = f(x)$. So, it is continuous at $x=2$.</p>",

    "<h4>ii) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\begin{cases} 2x^2+1 & x \\le 2 \\\\ 4x+1 & x > 2 \\end{cases} \\text{ at } x = 2$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} (2x^2+1) = 2 \\cdot 2^2 + 1 = 9$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} (4x+1) = 4 \\cdot 2 + 1 = 9$$",
    "$$f(2) = 2 \\cdot 2^2 + 1 = 9$$",
    "<p>Here, $\\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x) = f(x)$. So, it is continuous at $x=2$.</p>",

    "<h4>iii) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\begin{cases} 2x & x \\le 3 \\\\ 3x-3 & x > 3 \\end{cases} \\text{ at } x = 3$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 3^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 3^-} 2x = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 3^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 3^+} (3x-3) = 3 \\cdot 3 - 3 = 6$$",
    "$$f(3) = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6$$",
    "<p>Here, $\\lim_{x \\to 3} f(x) = f(x)$. So, it is continuous at $x=3$.</p>",

    "<h4>iv) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\begin{cases} 2x+1 & x < 1 \\\\ 2 & x = 1 \\\\ 3x & x > 1 \\end{cases} \\text{ at } x = 1$$",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 1^-} (2x+1) = 2 \\cdot 1 + 1 = 3$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 1^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 1^+} 3x = 3 \\cdot 1 = 3$$",
    "$$f(1) = 2$$",
    "<p>Here, $\\lim_{x \\to 1} f(x) \\ne f(1)$. So, it is discontinuous.</p>",

    "<h3>Question 3: Removable discontinuity and continuity adjustment</h3>",

    "<h4>i) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\begin{cases} x^2+2 & x<5 \\\\ 20 & x=5 \\\\ 3x+12 & x>5 \\end{cases}$$",
    "<p>Show that $f(x)$ has removable discontinuity at $x=5$.</p>",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 5^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 5^-} (x^2+2) = 5^2+2 = 27$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 5^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 5^+} (3x+12) = 3 \\cdot 5 + 12 = 27$$",
    "$$f(5) = 20$$",
    "<p>Here, discontinuity can be removed if $f(5) = 27$. So, it has removable discontinuity at $x=5$.</p>",

    "<h4>ii) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\begin{cases} 2x-3 & x<2 \\\\ 2 & x=2 \\\\ 3x-5 & x>2 \\end{cases}$$",
    "<p>Is the function $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2$? If not, how can the function $f(x)$ be made continuous at $x=2$?</p>",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} (2x-3) = 2 \\cdot 2 - 3 = 1$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} (3x-5) = 3 \\cdot 2 - 5 = 1$$",
    "$$f(2) = 2$$",
    "<p>Here, $\\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x) \\ne f(2)$. So, it is discontinuous at $x=2$.</p>",
    "<p>The function $f(x)$ can be made continuous at $x=2$ if $f(2) = 1$.</p>",

    "<h3>Question 4: Find the value of k for continuity</h3>",

    "<h4>i) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\begin{cases} 3x-1 & x<2 \\\\ kx+3 & x \\ge 2 \\end{cases}$$",
    "<p>Find the value of $k$ so that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$.</p>",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} (kx+3) = 2k+3$$",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} (3x-1) = 3 \\cdot 2 - 1 = 5$$",
    "<p>We know, $\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x)$:</p>",
    "$$2k+3 = 5$$",
    "$$k = \\frac{5-3}{2}$$",
    "$$\\therefore k = 1$$",

    "<h4>ii) Proof</h4>",
    "$$f(x) = \\begin{cases} \\dfrac{2x^2-18}{x-3} & x \\ne 3 \\\\ k & x=3 \\end{cases}$$",
    "<p>Find the value of $k$ so that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$.</p>",
    "<p><b>Solution:</b></p>",
    "$$\\lim_{x \\to 3} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 3} \\frac{2x^2-18}{x-3} = \\lim_{x \\to 3} \\frac{2(x+3)(x-3)}{x-3} = 2(3+3) = 12$$",
    "$$f(3) = k$$",
    "<p>We know, $\\lim_{x \\to 3} f(x) = f(3)$:</p>",
    "$$12 = k$$",
    "$$\\therefore k = 12$$"
  ]
}
